\section{Start From a Demand}

Eduction evaluates an intensional program by requesting a value of an identifier or expression at a context. The evaluator rewrites that demand into subsidiary demands, combines returned values, and normally warehouses completed results. This is demand-driven dataflow: demands can move upstream while values return downstream. It is an operational strategy for obtaining the Lucid denotation, not a replacement denotation.

Demand avoids computing irrelevant stream elements, but does not guarantee termination or small space. A future-dependent recurrence can create an unending chain of demands. A warehouse can retain a large context-indexed history, while omitting memoization can repeat expensive work. Parallel demand evaluation also needs a protocol for duplicate in-flight requests.

\section{Demand One Lucid Value}

Consider the Lucid equations:

\begin{verbatim}
x + y
where
  x = 0 fby x + 5;
  y = 1 fby y * 2;
end
\end{verbatim}

Demanding the result at index \(2\) creates demands for \(x@2\) and \(y@2\). They recursively reach their initialized cases and return \(10\) and \(4\), so the answer is \(14\). It does not require producing an infinite result stream. \Cref{fig:visual-360} distinguishes demand messages from returned values.

