% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Use a Warehouse and an In-Flight Table}

\begin{verbatim}
demand(node, context):
    key := (node, project_context(node, context))
    if warehouse contains key: return warehouse[key]
    if key is in_flight: wait for that computation
    mark key in_flight
    deps := expand_by_operator(node, context)
    values := demand dependencies, possibly in parallel
    result := apply_operator(node, values)
    publish warehouse[key] := result; clear in_flight
    return result
\end{verbatim}

The projected context is valid only when dimensional analysis proves that omitted dimensions cannot affect the node. Cyclic in-flight demands require semantic handling; simply waiting on oneself turns an unproductive definition into an opaque runtime deadlock.

\section{Preserve Demands Through Compilation}

An eductive IR needs equation/operator nodes, demand-dependency edges with context transforms, tag and value types, explicit index-shift state relationships, effect and partiality annotations, clock/dimension metadata, and source provenance. Compilation can produce an interpreter graph, bytecode for demand workers, warehouse schemas, or specialized memoized functions.

Legal optimizations include memoization, demand coalescing, parallel subsidiary demands, and context-key projection for pure expressions under their proven dependencies. Semantic-preservation risks include speculation that exposes otherwise undemanded errors or nontermination and caching effectful input that suppresses observations; eviction is denotationally safe for purity but can alter termination under finite resource limits. Validation should query the same expression/context pairs and compare values, errors, and definedness, rather than compare internal task order.

\section{Distinguish Shared Work From Circular Waiting}

For the preceding \texttt{x}, workers requesting \((x,2)\) and \((x,1)\) can share \((x,1)\). That dependency descends in time, so using the same node twice is not itself a cycle. By contrast, the equation \(z=z+1\) at a fixed context produces a wait from a key back to itself. Two equations can create the same problem indirectly: \((p,0)\) can ask for \((q,0)\), which asks for \((p,0)\) on another worker.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Request edge & In-flight relation & Treatment \\
\hline
\((x,2)\) to \((x,1)\) & Strictly earlier index & Share or start work \\
Two clients to \((x,1)\) & Common descendant & Join one promise \\
\((z,0)\) to \((z,0)\) & Self-loop & No productive result \\
\((q,0)\) to \((p,0)\) & Closes an existing path & Circular wait \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
await_dependency(parent, child):
    if ready(child): return result(child)
    atomically:
        if ready(child): return result(child)
        if parent == child or reachable(wait_graph, child, parent):
            return unresolved_cycle(parent, child)
        install_edge(wait_graph, parent, child)
        promise := start_or_join(child)
    try:
        return await_without_holding_graph_lock(promise)
    finally:
        remove_edge(wait_graph, parent, child)
\end{verbatim}

Treat the reported cycle as a diagnostic about this evaluation model, not as permission to return an arbitrary value for an unproductive source demand. If the source represents such a demand by undefinedness, tests should compare defined prefixes rather than mistake the diagnostic for a data token. A runtime must also publish completion before waking waiters and coordinate cancellation: dropping one client must not abandon a key another client still awaits. Finally, test a strictly descending recurrence at several indices in parallel. A detector keyed only by expression identity rejects that valid case even though each full demand key differs.

\paragraph{Distinguish a cycle from an unbounded chain.}
A demand for \((z,k)\) that requests \((z,k+1)\) repeatedly may not
terminate even though there is no cycle in the active wait graph.
Report a resource limit separately; cycle detection alone is not a
termination checker. For regression tests that deliberately exceed a
budget, prefer a deterministic bound on expanded keys over a wall-clock
timeout, since the latter can vary with the scheduler.
